WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.610
This is Safe World.

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:10.703
It blocks harmful websites and apps for your whole family, it is
completely free, and every decision is made on your own phone.

00:00:10.703 --> 00:00:14.282
Nothing about your browsing is ever sent anywhere.

00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:16.717
Here is what each part of it does.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:25.531
Safe World asks for a PIN before anything else, so protection
is never switched on with nothing guarding it.

00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:28.498
Straight after, it shows a recovery code.

00:00:28.498 --> 00:00:35.517
That is your only way back in if you forget the PIN,
so write it down and keep it somewhere safe.

00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:39.028
This one switch starts everything.

00:00:39.028 --> 00:00:44.050
While it is off, the app says plainly
that nothing is being blocked.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.379
It never pretends.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:55.350
Turn it on, and it tells you exactly how much is covered: over four
point four million harmful websites, kept up to date automatically.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.687
Ordinary browsing is untouched.

00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:01.778
Wikipedia opens exactly as it always did.

00:01:01.778 --> 00:01:08.035
But a real money card game site is refused,
and notice the page never loads at all.

00:01:08.035 --> 00:01:14.217
Blocking happens at the lookup stage, so
nothing from the site is ever downloaded.

00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:21.038
Social media, entertainment and games
are optional, and off by default.

00:01:21.038 --> 00:01:24.728
With the switch off, Facebook opens normally.

00:01:24.728 --> 00:01:29.156
Turn it on, and the blocked count jumps straight away.

00:01:29.156 --> 00:01:34.650
Same phone, same browser, nothing
else changed — now it is refused.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:40.496
Anything the categories do not cover, you can block by name.

00:01:40.496 --> 00:01:46.069
Separate them with commas, and subdomains
are included automatically.

00:01:46.069 --> 00:01:47.846
Adding a site is free.

00:01:47.846 --> 00:01:50.350
Removing one asks for your PIN.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:57.206
Some apps never make ordinary web lookups,
so blocking their website is not enough.

00:01:57.206 --> 00:02:08.213
Block apps stops the apps themselves from reaching the internet: social media,
streaming, and games and betting apps like Ludo, Teen Patti and Dream eleven.

00:02:08.213 --> 00:02:13.717
Messaging apps are never blocked, so a
protected phone can still reach family.

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:18.051
Here is the rule that explains the whole app.

00:02:18.051 --> 00:02:21.164
Making protection stronger is always free.

00:02:21.164 --> 00:02:23.535
Making it weaker costs your PIN.

00:02:23.535 --> 00:02:26.944
Turning a category on asks for nothing at all.

00:02:26.944 --> 00:02:33.317
But turning protection off asks for the PIN
you chose while you were thinking clearly.

00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:36.481
Two more pieces of friction.

00:02:36.481 --> 00:02:43.900
Always on VPN makes Android start Safe World itself,
and refuse to pass traffic while it is off.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.538
That is what stops another VPN app quietly switching it off.

00:02:48.538 --> 00:02:52.866
And uninstall protection makes the app harder to delete.

00:02:52.866 --> 00:02:57.117
It is friction, not a lock, and the app says so itself.

00:02:58.117 --> 00:03:09.535
In settings you can change the language — English, Bengali, Spanish or Arabic
— change your PIN, get a fresh recovery code, and check for updates.

00:03:09.535 --> 00:03:16.417
The always allow list wins over every category,
so changing it asks for the PIN as well.

00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:18.889
That is Safe World.

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:26.948
Free to download, no account to create, and nothing
about your family's browsing ever leaves the device.

00:03:26.948 --> 00:03:32.450
The full written guide, with every screen
explained, is on the website.
